ROUND 7 -- the three items the notes flag as open
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

  [x] Return-time tail bound (ReturnTail.lean). For every lam in (1, phi),
      the set of window points whose discarded child needs more than t
      forced steps to re-enter the window has Lebesgue measure at most
      2 g / lam^(t+1) (return_time_tail). At lam = 3/2 this is
      <= (2/3)^(t+2) (return_time_tail_three_halves), i.e.
      <= (4/3)(2/3)^t times the measure of the window
      (return_time_tail_prob_three_halves).
  [x] Renewal inequality: proved in deterministic form. Inside an interval
      with the doubling property, the localized count of surviving words at
      least doubles every T steps (ExpCount.two_mul_kappa_le), which is what
      the exponential bound uses. The empirical renewal inequality of the
      notes, with the measured return-time law, is not proved.
  [x] Exponential lower bound for K_m. At lam = 3/2:
      2^(m/5) <= K (3/2) m (ExpLower.two_pow_le_K). Uniformly over the
      common window: 2^(m/8) <= K lam m for every lam in [1000/667, 8/5]
      (ExpWindow.two_pow_le_K_window). The same argument at multiplicity k
      (ExpMulti.pow_le_K_of_mdoubling) sharpens the lam = 3/2 bound to
      15^(m/12) <= K (3/2) m (ExpSharp.fifteen_pow_le_K), i.e. growth 1.2532
      per step against the measured 1.3333.

  How, in one paragraph: a branch word issued from x in (0,1) is legal
  exactly when its final image is again in (0,1) (absorption), so only
  images have to be controlled. If every point of a closed interval J
  inside (0,1) containing 1/2 has two distinct branch words of a common
  length T whose images stay in J, then the minimum over J of the number of
  surviving words of length n at least doubles every T steps, giving
  2^(m/T) <= K lam m. The doubling property is certified by a finite
  rational computation: cells tiling J, each carrying two words, checked by
  a one-sided interval enclosure that is monotone in the point and in the
